// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// A CLOSURE THAT LEAVES ITS FRAME TAKES ITS CAPTURES WITH IT, AND EACH IS RELEASED ONCE
// (`@FR-L-CapOwn`, with `@FR-L-CapOne`, `@FR-L-CapRef` and `@FR-B-View`).  A closure record that
// leaves its frame takes over the release of what it captured, its cascade is its reach, and
// whoever holds it releases it: each kind of capture is freed once, by the record; a lambda handed
// out by an explicit `return` inside a branch is delivered, not freed by its frame (loft#1477); a
// leaving closure over an element view keeps the vector's store alive (loft#2154); and a frame
// that takes over a leaving closure releases it, a frame answering text included (loft#2282).
// Four sections, each with its cells and receipt.
//
// ── a-leaving-closure-releases-each-kind-of-capture-once ──
//
// `@FR-L-CapOwn` / `@FR-L-CapOne` / `@FR-L-CapRef` — A LEAVING CLOSURE RELEASES EACH KIND OF
// CAPTURE ONCE.
//
// `(L-CapOwn)`: a closure record that leaves its frame takes over the release of what it
// captured, and its CASCADE is its reach — each capture is freed once, by the record, and the
// frame frees none of them.  Where the frame never owned the store, the record has nothing to
// take over and must free nothing: a parameter is the caller's (`(L-CapRef)` for `&S`), a
// constant is the program's.
//
// The cases vary one axis each.  WHAT is captured: a hash, a sorted and an index collection, a
// closure that itself captured a struct (the cascade reaches the inner record), a tuple holding
// a struct, a nullable struct present and absent.  HOW the record leaves: the return, a struct
// field, two records over ONE store in one struct (`(L-CapOne)`: one owns, both leave).  WHERE
// the capture came from: a by-value parameter, a `&S` parameter, a call's result, a constant.
// The caller reads its own value after the closure is gone.  Each runs three rounds, so a
// double release or a leak shows on the store-lifetime channel as well.  Every value is worked
// out by hand.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard: every cell answered as the rule says, on both
//   backends, on the tree it landed on; the defects this pass found are 2154 and 2155 beside it.
// CHANNEL: the asserts, and the store-lifetime channel under LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1.
// ARMED: LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1 LOFT_POISON=1, which the suite's leak sweep arms.
// WITNESS: each message names the capture and prints the answer.
// HOLDS: every assertion passes on both backends; no store is reported never freed or read after free.
//
// Held FIXED, so NOT measured here: capturing closures in collections (refused by @C116), and
// the `&fn` link route (1609 beside it).
//
// ── 1477-an-explicit-return-of-a-lambda-keeps-its-capture ──
//
// loft#1477 — a capturing lambda handed out by an EXPLICIT `return` standing inside a branch.
//
// `@FR-L-CapOwn` — a captured heap store is freed once, by whichever of the record and the frame
// outlives the other, and a record the return DELIVERS is the caller's.  Deciding that needs
// "which closure records can this return deliver", and `returned_closure_records` answers it two
// ways: the tail walks a stack that reads a `Value::FnRef`, while every `Value::Return` in the
// body routes through `collect_return_sources`, whose arms are `Var`, `Block`, `Return`/`Drop`,
// `Insert`, `If`, `Span` — and `_ => {}`.
//
// That decoder answers in VARIABLES, and a capturing lambda is not one: it is a `FnRef` naming
// its `___clos_N` directly.  So `return fn() { … }` written straight out contributed nothing,
// the record was not in the delivered set, and the frame freed what it had just handed over —
// which cascades into the capture.  The tail path would have caught it, but it only reaches a
// block's LAST operator, so it sees the trailing `return` and never one standing earlier in an
// arm.  One question, two decoders, and the blind one on this route.
//
// Same family as loft#1474, where the record local's free still read the declared return's
// overwritten `CalleeFrame` note after the fn-ref variable's free had been moved off it.  The
// recurring shape is worth more than either fix: when one question has several decoders, the
// defect sits in whichever of them the failing route happens to consult.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  where the `return` STANDS (an `if` arm · a `match` arm · nested two
//                        deep · a loop body · the trailing one, which was always right) ·
//                        whether the lambda CAPTURES, since a bare one carries no record and
//                        cannot show this · how many arms return · and whether the two arms
//                        capture the SAME local or different ones, because the delivered set is
//                        per RECORD and a shared store is a different question.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the caller CALLS the returned lambda — an uncalled one reads nothing
//                        and the released capture stays invisible.  And the frame owns a heap
//                        local, without which there is no scope-exit free to get this wrong.
//
// @falsified-at: ab951b7d3, WITH THE INSTRUMENTS ARMED — `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1 LOFT_POISON=1
//   make falsify`: interpret exit 1 -> 0 and `kt=82 E77×4` leaked -> clean, native the same.
//   The control's own value channel already shows it — `e1` answers `null` where `102` is
//   wanted, and the instrument names the cause (`USE AFTER FREE (read) store #4 type=E77`,
//   created / freed / read-now pcs all reported).  The trailing-`return` control is INERT
//   either side, which is the point: only the branch-borne `return` takes the blind route.
//   HOLDS: exit, assertions, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.
//
// ── 2154-a-leaving-closure-over-an-element-view-keeps-the-vectors-store ──
//
// loft#2154, `@FR-L-CapOwn` / `@FR-B-View` / `@FR-O-Latest` — A LEAVING CLOSURE OVER AN ELEMENT
// VIEW KEEPS THE VECTOR'S STORE.
//
// `y = vs[1]` is a view (`(B-View)`): it names a place inside the store `vs` reaches, and `vs`
// is itself a view of the store its literal minted.  A closure capturing `y` shares that store
// (`(L-CapHeap)`), and a closure that LEAVES its frame takes the store's release over
// (`(L-CapOwn)`), so the frame must not free it.  The frame looked one view deep: it gave up
// the release of `vs`, which owns nothing, and freed the store under the closure.  Returned
// directly, the closure then read a freed store; returned in a struct field, it read `null`.
//
// The cases vary one axis each: how the record leaves (the return, a struct field), what the
// element holds (a heap member, scalars only), the index, where the vector came from (a literal,
// a call), a nested projection (`qs[0].s`), and what happens to the vector after the build (a
// rebind, an append) — the record keeps the store it was built with (`(L-CapRebind)`).  The
// controls beside them passed before: a field view, the closure called in its frame, the whole
// vector captured, an element of a vector parameter, an element reached through a struct field.
// Each runs three rounds, so a double release or a leak also shows on the store-lifetime
// channel.  Every value is worked out by hand.
//
// @falsified-at: b36d0449f — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0,
//   native 2 assertion failures -> 0, native panicked -> clean (the installed main as the control)
// CHANNEL: assertion failures and store panics, both backends.
// WITNESS: on b36d0449f `test_the_return` and `test_the_vector_after_the_build` hit `Store access out of
//   bounds: rec=3735928559` (the poison pattern: a freed store read) and `test_a_struct_field`
//   answers `left in a struct field: got null, want 202`; `test_the_controls` passes there too.
// HOLDS: every assertion passes on both backends here; no store reported read after free or never freed.
// ARMED: LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1 LOFT_POISON=1 turn the freed read into a reported use-after-free.
//
// Held FIXED, so NOT measured here: keyed collections (an element of a hash reached through `??`
// is a copy), and a record left behind in its frame.
//
// ── 2282-a-frame-answering-text-releases-the-closure-it-holds ──
//
// loft#2282, `@FR-L-CapOwn` — A FRAME ANSWERING TEXT RELEASES THE CLOSURE IT HOLDS.
//
// `g = one()` takes over a closure that LEFT `one`, so `g`'s frame owns the record and its
// capture, and releases both when `g` goes.  In a function that answers TEXT it never did: the
// sweep read the declared return's deps, which number ATTRIBUTES, as frame variables, and the
// hidden text buffer's attribute index named a local.  Where that local was the fn-ref, the
// fn-ref read as the returned value and was never released.  One record and its capture leaked
// per call, quietly.  Each capture here has a drop hook, so a missed release shows as a missing
// `D` in the trace.
//
// Every expected value is hand-computed.  `k1` and `k2` are controls: shapes that were right
// before.
//
// CHANNEL: assertion failures (the trace), every backend.
// HOLDS: clean under `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1` on all three backends.

struct S { v: integer, w: vector<integer> }
struct E { k: text, v: integer }
struct Box { f: fn() -> integer, k: integer }
struct Two { a: fn() -> integer, b: fn() -> integer }

GV = [4, 5, 6];

fn check(name: text, got: integer, want: integer) { assert(got == want, "{name}: got {got}, want {want}"); }

fn of_hash(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  h: hash<E[k]> = [E { k: "a", v: n }, E { k: "b", v: n * 2 }];
  fn() -> integer { len(h) * 100 + (h["b"] ?? E { k: "z", v: -1 }).v }
}
fn of_sorted(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  s: sorted<E[k]> = [E { k: "b", v: n * 3 }, E { k: "a", v: n }];
  fn() -> integer { len(s) * 100 + (s["b"] ?? E { k: "z", v: -1 }).v }
}
fn of_index(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  x: index<E[k]> = [E { k: "c", v: n * 5 }, E { k: "a", v: n }];
  fn() -> integer { len(x) * 100 + (x["c"] ?? E { k: "z", v: -1 }).v }
}
fn of_closure(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  s = S { v: n, w: [1, 2] };
  inner = fn() -> integer { s.v + len(s.w) };
  fn() -> integer { inner() * 10 }
}
fn of_tuple(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  t = (S { v: n, w: [1, 2, 3] }, n * 2);
  fn() -> integer { t.0.v * 100 + len(t.0.w) * 10 + t.1 }
}
fn of_nullable(n: integer, present: boolean) -> fn() -> integer {
  s: S? = null;
  if present { s = S { v: n, w: [n] }; }
  fn() -> integer { if s == null { -1 } else { (s ?? S { v: 0, w: [] }).v } }
}
fn in_a_field(n: integer) -> Box {
  s = S { v: n, w: [n, n + 1] };
  Box { f: fn() -> integer { s.v * 10 + len(s.w) }, k: n }
}
fn two_over_one(n: integer) -> Two {
  s = S { v: n, w: [n, n, n] };
  Two { a: fn() -> integer { s.v }, b: fn() -> integer { len(s.w) * 100 + s.v } }
}
fn of_value_param(s: S) -> fn() -> integer { fn() -> integer { s.v * 10 + len(s.w) } }
fn of_ref_param(s: &S) -> fn() -> integer { s.w += [0]; fn() -> integer { s.v * 10 + len(s.w) } }
fn build(n: integer) -> S { S { v: n, w: [n, n] } }
fn of_call(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer { s = build(n); fn() -> integer { s.v * 100 + len(s.w) } }
fn of_constant(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer { fn() -> integer { len(GV) * 10 + n } }

fn test_what_is_captured() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    a = of_hash(r);
    check("a hash", a(), 200 + r * 2);
    b = of_sorted(r);
    check("a sorted", b(), 200 + r * 3);
    c = of_index(r);
    check("an index", c(), 200 + r * 5);
    d = of_closure(r);
    check("a closure", d(), (r + 2) * 10);
    check("a closure, again", d(), (r + 2) * 10);
    e = of_tuple(r);
    check("a tuple", e(), r * 100 + 30 + r * 2);
    f = of_nullable(r, true);
    check("a present nullable", f(), r);
    g = of_nullable(r, false);
    check("an absent nullable", g(), -1);
  }
}

fn test_how_it_leaves() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    bx = in_a_field(r);
    g = bx.f;
    check("in a struct field", g() * 10 + bx.k, (r * 10 + 2) * 10 + r);
    t = two_over_one(r);
    a = t.a;
    b = t.b;
    check("two over one store", a() * 1000 + b(), r * 1000 + 300 + r);
  }
}

fn test_where_it_came_from() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    p = S { v: r, w: [1, 2] };
    f = of_value_param(p);
    check("a by-value parameter", f(), r * 10 + 2);
    p.w += [3];
    check("the caller's struct", len(p.w), 3);
    q = S { v: r, w: [1] };
    g = of_ref_param(q);
    check("a & parameter", g(), r * 10 + 2);
    q.w += [9];
    check("shared with the caller", g(), r * 10 + 3);
    h = of_call(r);
    check("a call's result", h(), r * 100 + 2);
    k = of_constant(r);
    check("a constant", k(), 30 + r);
  }
  check("the constant afterwards", len(GV), 3);
}

struct E77 { a: integer }
enum W77 { One, S2Two }

// e1 — the filed shape: an explicit `return` in an `if` arm, two arms over two locals.
fn if_arm(p: boolean) -> fn() -> integer {
  e: E77 = E77 { a: 2 };
  if p { return fn() -> integer { 100 + e.a }; }
  return fn() -> integer { 204 };
}

// e2 — both arms capturing the SAME local.  The delivered set is per RECORD, so a shared store
// asks `(L-CapOne)`'s question as well as this one, and the two must both hold.
fn same_local(p: boolean) -> fn() -> integer {
  e: E77 = E77 { a: 2 };
  if p { return fn() -> integer { 100 + e.a }; }
  return fn() -> integer { 200 + e.a };
}

// e3 — a `match` arm rather than an `if` arm.
fn match_arm(p: W77) -> fn() -> integer {
  e: E77 = E77 { a: 2 };
  match p { One => { return fn() -> integer { 100 + e.a }; }, S2Two => { } }
  return fn() -> integer { 204 };
}

// e4 — nested two deep, so the `return` is neither the block's last operator nor one level in.
fn nested(p: boolean, q: boolean = true) -> fn() -> integer {
  e: E77 = E77 { a: 2 };
  if p {
    if q { return fn() -> integer { 100 + e.a }; }
    return fn() -> integer { 300 + e.a };
  }
  return fn() -> integer { 204 };
}

// e5 — the `return` sits in a LOOP body.
fn loop_body(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  e: E77 = E77 { a: 2 };
  for i in 0..n { if i + e.a > 0 { return fn() -> integer { 100 + e.a }; } }
  return fn() -> integer { 204 };
}

// e6 — THREE returning arms, so a fix that found only the first would still be short.
fn three_returns(p: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  x: E77 = E77 { a: 1 };
  if p == 0 { return fn() -> integer { 100 + x.a }; }
  if p == 1 { return fn() -> integer { 201 + x.a }; }
  return fn() -> integer { 302 + x.a };
}

// c1 — CONTROL: the TRAILING `return`, which the tail path always reached.  Inert either side.
fn ctl_trailing(p: boolean) -> fn() -> integer {
  d: E77 = E77 { a: 4 };
  if p && d.a > 0 { return fn() -> integer { 200 + d.a }; }
  return fn() -> integer { 200 + d.a };
}

// c2 — CONTROL: a NON-capturing lambda from a branch `return`.  It carries no record, so there
// is nothing for the frame to free wrongly; a cure that fired here would be reaching too wide.
fn ctl_bare(p: boolean) -> fn() -> integer {
  e: E77 = E77 { a: 2 };
  if p && e.a > 0 { return fn() -> integer { 102 }; }
  return fn() -> integer { 204 };
}

fn test_an_explicit_return_of_a_lambda_keeps_its_capture() {
  g1 = if_arm(true);  h1 = if_arm(false);
  assert(g1() == 102, "e1 explicit return in an `if` arm: {g1()}");
  assert(h1() == 204, "e1b the trailing return, bare: {h1()}");

  g2 = same_local(true); h2 = same_local(false);
  assert(g2() == 102, "e2 both arms over one local, branch return: {g2()}");
  assert(h2() == 202, "e2b both arms over one local, trailing: {h2()}");

  g3 = match_arm(W77.One); h3 = match_arm(W77.S2Two);
  assert(g3() == 102, "e3 explicit return in a `match` arm: {g3()}");
  assert(h3() == 204, "e3b match arm not taken: {h3()}");

  a4 = nested(true, true); b4 = nested(true, false); c4 = nested(false);
  assert(a4() == 102, "e4 nested, inner return: {a4()}");
  assert(b4() == 302, "e4b nested, second return in the same arm: {b4()}");
  assert(c4() == 204, "e4c nested, trailing: {c4()}");

  g5 = loop_body(3); h5 = loop_body(0);
  assert(g5() == 102, "e5 return from a loop body: {g5()}");
  assert(h5() == 204, "e5b zero iterations, trailing: {h5()}");

  a6 = three_returns(0); b6 = three_returns(1); c6 = three_returns(2);
  assert(a6() == 101, "e6 first of three returns: {a6()}");
  assert(b6() == 202, "e6b second of three returns: {b6()}");
  assert(c6() == 303, "e6c trailing of three: {c6()}");

  g7 = ctl_trailing(true); h7 = ctl_trailing(false);
  assert(g7() == 204, "c1 both returns capture one local, branch: {g7()}");
  assert(h7() == 204, "c1b both returns capture one local, trailing: {h7()}");
  g8 = ctl_bare(true); h8 = ctl_bare(false);
  assert(g8() == 102, "c2 non-capturing from a branch return: {g8()}");
  assert(h8() == 204, "c2b non-capturing, trailing: {h8()}");

  println("1477-an-explicit-return-of-a-lambda-keeps-its-capture ok");
}

struct P { v: integer }
struct Q { s: S }
struct Bag { items: vector<S> }
struct S3Box { f: fn() -> integer }


fn two(n: integer) -> vector<S> { [S { v: n, w: [1] }, S { v: n + 1, w: [1, 2] }] }

fn returned(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer { vs = two(n); y = vs[1]; fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) } }
fn literal(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  vs = [S { v: n, w: [1] }, S { v: n + 1, w: [1, 2] }];
  y = vs[1];
  fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) }
}
fn first(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  vs = [S { v: n, w: [1] }, S { v: n + 1, w: [1, 2] }];
  y = vs[0];
  fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) }
}
fn scalars(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer { vs = [P { v: n }, P { v: n + 1 }]; y = vs[1]; fn() -> integer { y.v } }
fn s3_nested(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  qs = [Q { s: S { v: n, w: [1, 2, 3] } }];
  z = qs[0].s;
  fn() -> integer { z.v * 100 + len(z.w) }
}
fn boxed(n: integer) -> S3Box {
  vs = [S { v: n, w: [1] }, S { v: n + 1, w: [1, 2] }];
  y = vs[1];
  S3Box { f: fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) } }
}
fn rebound(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  vs = [S { v: n, w: [1] }, S { v: n + 1, w: [1, 2] }];
  y = vs[1];
  f = fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) };
  vs = [S { v: 50, w: [] }];
  check("the vector rebound", len(vs), 1);
  f
}
fn appended(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  vs = [S { v: n, w: [1] }, S { v: n + 1, w: [1, 2] }];
  y = vs[1];
  f = fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) };
  vs += [S { v: 60, w: [] }];
  f
}

// Controls.
fn field_view(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  q = Q { s: S { v: n, w: [1, 2, 3] } };
  x = q.s;
  fn() -> integer { x.v * 100 + len(x.w) }
}
fn in_frame(n: integer) -> integer {
  vs = two(n);
  y = vs[1];
  f = fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) };
  f()
}
fn whole(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer { vs = two(n); fn() -> integer { vs[1].v * 100 + len(vs[1].w) } }
fn of_param(vs: vector<S>) -> fn() -> integer { y = vs[1]; fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) } }
fn through_field(n: integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  bg = Bag { items: two(n) };
  y = bg.items[1];
  fn() -> integer { y.v * 100 + len(y.w) }
}

fn test_the_return() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    f = returned(r);
    check("a call's vector", f(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    check("read twice", f(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    g = literal(r);
    check("a literal vector", g(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    h = first(r);
    check("index 0", h(), r * 100 + 1);
    k = scalars(r);
    check("an element with no heap member", k(), r + 1);
    m = s3_nested(r);
    check("a s3_nested projection", m(), r * 100 + 3);
  }
}

fn test_a_struct_field() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    b = boxed(r);
    g = b.f;
    check("left in a struct field", g(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
  }
}

fn test_the_vector_after_the_build() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    f = rebound(r);
    check("the store it was built with", f(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    g = appended(r);
    check("after an append", g(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
  }
}

fn test_the_controls() {
  for r in 1..4 {
    a = field_view(r);
    check("a field view", a(), r * 100 + 3);
    check("called in its frame", in_frame(r), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    c = whole(r);
    check("the whole vector", c(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    vs = two(r);
    d = of_param(vs);
    check("an element of a parameter", d(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
    check("the caller's vector", len(vs), 2);
    e = through_field(r);
    check("through a struct field", e(), (r + 1) * 100 + 2);
  }
}

struct H { id: integer }

fn OpDrop(self: H) { note("D{self.id}"); }
fn mkh(id: integer) -> H { note("M{id}"); H { id: id } }

fn trace_path() -> text { return "loft_2282_text_trace.tmp"; }

fn note(s: text) {
  f = file(trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  if cur != "" { cur += ","; }
  cur += s;
  bytes: vector<u8> = [];
  for c in cur { bytes += [c as u8 ?? 0]; }
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), bytes) { println("trace write failed"); }
}

fn drain() -> text {
  f = file(trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), empty) { println("trace clear failed"); }
  return cur;
}

fn ok(nm: text, got: text, want: text) {
  assert(got == want, "{nm}: {got}, want {want}");
}

fn one(id: integer) -> fn() -> integer { e = mkh(id); fn() -> integer { 3 + e.id } }

// The issue's shape: the fn-ref is the frame's first local.
fn c1() -> text { g = one(2); v = g(); "{v}" }
// Called inside the text it answers.
fn c2() -> text { g = one(3); "{g()}" }
// An explicit `return` in a branch, and the tail.
fn c3(big: boolean) -> text {
  g = one(4);
  if big { return "big {g()}"; }
  "small"
}
// A `text?` answer: the same buffer.
fn c4(none: boolean) -> text? {
  g = one(5);
  if none { return null; }
  "{g()}"
}
// A parameter before the fn-ref.
fn c5(n: integer) -> text { g = one(6); "{g() + n}" }

// Controls: an integer answer, and the fn-ref not the first local.
fn k1() -> integer { g = one(7); g() }
fn k2() -> text { z = 1; g = one(8); "{g() + z}" }

fn test_a_frame_answering_text_releases_the_closure_it_holds() {
  drain();
  ok("c1 value          ", c1(), "5");
  ok("c1 released       ", drain(), "M2,D2");
  ok("c2 value          ", c2(), "6");
  ok("c2 released       ", drain(), "M3,D3");
  ok("c3 return value   ", c3(true), "big 7");
  ok("c3 return released", drain(), "M4,D4");
  ok("c3 tail value     ", c3(false), "small");
  ok("c3 tail released  ", drain(), "M4,D4");
  ok("c4 text value     ", c4(false) ?? "none", "8");
  ok("c4 text released  ", drain(), "M5,D5");
  ok("c4 null value     ", c4(true) ?? "none", "none");
  ok("c4 null released  ", drain(), "M5,D5");
  ok("c5 value          ", c5(10), "19");
  ok("c5 released       ", drain(), "M6,D6");
  ok("k1 value          ", "{k1()}", "10");
  ok("k1 released       ", drain(), "M7,D7");
  ok("k2 value          ", k2(), "12");
  ok("k2 released       ", drain(), "M8,D8");
  delete(trace_path());
  println("a frame answering text releases the closure it holds: every cell holds");
}
